  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Slaughter Moon was a blood-ritual performed once by orca war-bands during the Orca Incursion, the coordinated assault that nearly broke both penguin civilizations on The Berg. It was not a recurring celebration. The orcas performed it once, and the evidence suggests once was sufficient.
    
    ## The Ritual
    
    Orca shamans, led by the war-shaman Thung Thrakrek, carved runes into their own flesh and packed the wounds with crushed aurora-crystal stolen from Aurora Stack's outer spires. The chant — described by every surviving witness identically — did not originate from the shamans' throats but from beneath the ice, as though The Berg itself were responding. The ritual was timed to the Long Dark, exploiting the veil-thinning that amplifies both aurora-practice and pressure-depth magic.
    
    Its immediate purpose was the consecration of Grungruk's Claw, the blackened whale-bone trident wielded by war-leader Zund Gnasharkin. Its broader effects were worse. Slide Prism — a Clever Ice Age harvesting technique that fragments aurora-light into prismatic beams — amplified the ritual's reach across the frozen sea. The blood-magic combat techniques known as the Rupture-Song and Khrrak's Collapse were enhanced beyond what individual scarification could produce.
    
    Penguin prisoners — Aurora Stack scouts, Nightshelf miners, surface dwellers seized near beacon-fires — were forced to observe. Most did not survive. The survivors' accounts agree on Thung Thrakrek's transformation: scars luminous with captured aurora-light, voice carrying a weight the witnesses struggled to articulate.
    
    ## Scholarly Assessment
    
    Qingqe'frostlings researchers studying the aftermath documented that the chant's sub-ice resonance resists classification within standard Aurora frameworks — a point their treatises note without explanation, which is at least honest.
    
    The casualty figures among the forced observers are not recorded with any precision. I note this not to dispute them but because no one thought to count.
